# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` event consumer of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}:
an EventBridge rule that sends events to an AWS Lambda function in
TypeScript, which stores the item each carries in a DynamoDB table, with its
infrastructure as a CDK app. Scaffolded by C4 Factory.

## The events

The rule is on the account's default event bus and matches the events
whose `source` is `{{ .Container }}` and whose `detail-type` is
`ItemSubmitted`: `eventPatternFor` in `infra/event_stack.ts` says which,
and the stack's `EventPattern` output repeats it. A producer puts one with
`PutEvents`, its `detail` an item:

```json
{
  "Source": "{{ .Container }}",
  "DetailType": "ItemSubmitted",
  "Detail": "{\"id\": \"lamp-1\", \"name\": \"Lamp\", \"quantity\": 3}"
}
```

The event's `id`, which EventBridge gives it, is its key. The rule and
Lambda deliver an event at least once; an event delivered again with the
same id is stored once. A producer that puts the same item twice puts two
events, and the item is simply put twice. Here an event stores its item:
put the consumer's own work in `consume` in `src/consume.ts`.

The rule tries to hand an event to Lambda for an hour, 10 times at most. An
event that throws is run again by Lambda twice, a minute and then two
apart. An event that still fails, or that the rule could not hand over,
waits 14 days in the dead-letter queue, where an alarm tells you;
`docs/runbook.md` says how to run it again. An event whose `detail` is no
item fails the same way, so a person sees it.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci           # install the exact packages in package-lock.json
npm test         # unit tests and the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run synth    # write the development CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth runs offline, bundles the
function with the installed esbuild, and needs neither Docker nor credentials.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production. Each consumes the events of its own account and region.
Production keeps the tables and the dead-letter queue when the stack goes,
protects the tables and the stack from deletion, recovers them to any second
of the last 35 days, backs them up daily with AWS Backup, traces with X-Ray
and keeps logs a year; development throws its data away with the stack and
keeps logs a month. Both tiers alarm and have a dashboard.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which C4 Factory renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. The alert email is the
stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which a deploy can set per environment. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the rule's ARN, `EventPattern`, what a
producer's events must match, `FunctionName`, the function the rule invokes,
and `AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its alarms notify.

Deploying (`cdk deploy`, after `cdk bootstrap` of the account) is for its
owner, with their own credentials.

## Layout

- `src/handler.ts` is the Lambda entry point: it creates the DynamoDB client
  and Powertools' Logger and Metrics once, at module scope, reads
  `TABLE_NAME` and `IDEMPOTENCY_TABLE_NAME`, and runs `consume` once per
  event id through Powertools' Idempotency, after checking the event.
- `src/consume.ts` checks the event and stores its item; `src/items.ts`
  holds the rules, free of AWS; `src/item_store.ts` keeps items in DynamoDB
  behind the `ItemStore` interface tests fake.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/event_stack.ts` is
  the main one: the rule and its pattern, the function with its log group
  and role, the dead-letter queue, the tables, the alarms (`infra/alarms.ts`)
  and, in production, the backups (`infra/backups.ts`). The stack is named
  after the package. `infra/operations.ts` holds what the Container
  promises.
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
